chip_platform
version: 2.24.0 (environment-specific paths removed)
date: 2026-08-01
changes:
  - Removed all hardcoded personal and machine-specific paths from the tree.
      * the pristine-tree guards in ci/run_regress.sh, setup_custom_riscv.sh,
        verif/build_all.sh and every per-case run.sh rejected $HOME/chipyard and
        $HOME/chipyard-benchmark by name. That test is meaningless on any other
        machine and blocked legitimate trees, so it is gone.
      * CHIPYARD, CY, RISCV, DV, VENV and STUB no longer default to a specific
        home directory. They now use ${VAR:?message} so an unset variable fails
        immediately with a clear message instead of silently pointing somewhere
        that does not exist.
      * .vscode/launch.json and tasks.json now reference ${env:RISCV} and
        ${env:CY} rather than absolute paths.
      * In-tree documentation uses placeholders in place of absolute paths.
      * The tree directory is named chip_platform. The earlier
        chip_platform_verif_noko name was a temporary label from the
        Korean-removal pass and no longer appears anywhere.
      * vp/spike/verif_* removed from the tree. They are compiled host x86-64
        binaries carrying an absolute RUNPATH, so a copy from another machine is
        never usable and, worse, run_regress.sh will not rebuild a file that
        exists - leaving a stale libriscv silently in use. They are now built by
        setup_custom_riscv.sh --rebuild-vp and listed in .gitignore.

  - Platform/Common/Inc/verif_mbx_base.h added. One base address, VERIF_MBX_BASE,
    with every mailbox offset derived from it. Default 0x80010000 is unchanged,
    so RV32RocketConfig targets behave exactly as before.

    Derived from it now:
      Platform/Common/Inc/util.h              BREAK_POINT_ADDR
      Platform/Common/Inc/verif_ca.h          cycle/instret mirrors, CA table
      verif/backend/chipyard/verif_addr_chipyard.h   host-side equivalents
      verif/ip/S5740/WFI/.../WFI_hostAccess.h TEST_DEBUG_BASE_ADDR
      verif/ip/S5740/BENCH/smoke/Src/BENCH_smoke.c   scratch word

    To relocate on a target where 0x80010000 is not writable data memory:
      EXTRA_CFLAGS="-DPRELOAD -DVERIF_MBX_BASE=0x80038000u"
    The host build needs the same value; the offsets are duplicated in
    verif_addr_chipyard.h because host and DUT share no include path.

    Why: bp_init() stores to BREAK_POINT_ADDR on the boot path, before main.
    On SCRConfig the default lands in ITIM and the store faults, so nothing
    else gets a chance to run. Per-test headers still carry their own literals
    and are listed in HARDCODED_ADDRESS_AUDIT.md.

  - Platform/Chipset/TinyRocket/link.vwp3.ld added. ITIM holds .text only;
    .rodata, .data, .bss, heap and stack all go to DTIM.
      code 0x80000000 64K   (ITIM, instruction fetch only)
      data 0x80020000 128K  (DTIM)

    Why link.vwp2.ld was not enough: it left .rodata in ITIM on the assumption
    that read-only data belongs with read-only code. GCC compiles a switch into
    a jump table in .rodata and reads it with a plain load, so that load hits
    instruction memory and faults. Observed in CoreMark get_seed_32 at cycle
    2794 (0x800026a2), trapping to _trap_vector_entry and ending in
    exit(CODE_FATAL). This matches mem_test.riscv, which keeps only 6.5K of code
    at 0x80000000 and places all 17K of everything else at 0x80020000.

  - Platform/Common/Inc/util.h: BREAK_POINT_ADDR wrapped in #ifndef so the host
    mailbox can be relocated per target. On SCRConfig the stock 0x80010000 falls
    inside ITIM. Build with e.g.
      EXTRA_CFLAGS="-DPRELOAD -DBREAK_POINT_ADDR=0x80038000"

  - Platform/Chipset/TinyRocket/link.vwp2.ld added: second VWP variant, using
    only the two memory regions the working sample on that machine uses.
      0x80000000  code (text/rodata)                        32K
      0x80020000  tohost/fromhost, data, bss, heap, stack   64K
      0x80002000..0x8001FFFF  deliberately unused
    Select it per build:
      make -f Implementation/Makefile.VERIF ... \
           LDSCRIPT=Platform/Chipset/TinyRocket/link.vwp2.ld

    Why: with link.vwp.ld the harness did read our exit code, so HTIF works -
    but the firmware exited with CODE_FATAL (2) after about 23 ms of simulated
    time, which is the trap handler reporting an unexpected exception during
    boot. The stock layout assumes one contiguous 60K region and puts the stack
    at 0x8000F000, inside the gap that mem_test.riscv avoids. If that range is
    not backed by memory on this SoC, the first stack push faults.
    HYPOTHESIS - the SoC memory map has not been confirmed.

  - Platform/Chipset/TinyRocket/link.vwp.ld added: VWP/HPC (xcelium) variant of
    link.ld that pins tohost/fromhost to fixed addresses.
      tohost   0x80020000
      fromhost 0x80020040
    Select it per build:
      make -f Implementation/Makefile.VERIF ... \
           LDSCRIPT=Platform/Chipset/TinyRocket/link.vwp.ld
    link.ld itself is UNCHANGED, so dscloud behaviour is not affected.

    Why: a CoreMark ELF ran 582 s under xcelium and exited normally with no
    console output. A working sample there (mem_test.riscv) has tohost at
    0x80020000 while the stock script left ours at 0x80004148, which suggests
    the harness watches a fixed address rather than resolving the ELF symbol.
    NOT yet confirmed with the hardware team - see HTIF_ADDRESS_QUESTIONS.md.
    The 0x40 gap between tohost and fromhost is reproduced on the assumption
    that it is required.

  - NOKO_CLEANUP.md moved to chip_docs_verif/verif/. It is a record of the
    Korean-removal pass, not something a user of the tree needs; the tree keeps
    only what is required to build and run.
  - env.sh removed from the tree root. It was a 0-byte stub and nothing
    referenced it - every "env.sh" in the scripts refers to $CHIPYARD/env.sh.
  - CUSTOM_TOOLCHAIN.md stays: it is the usage document for
    setup_custom_riscv.sh, which sits next to it.

  - verif/ip/S5740/CA/measure/ca_compare3.py now accepts both "[verif]" (chipyard
    host) and "[verif_spike]" (verif_spike_run) table prefixes. Previously a
    model log had to be rewritten with sed before it could be compared.
  - verif/ip/S5740/CA/measure/spike_compare.py removed. ca_compare3.py is a
    superset: pass '-' as the first argument for the two-way view it used to
    provide, or three logs for the full comparison.
  - verif/ip/S5740/I2SR/int_muldiv/Vseq/I2SR_int_muldiv.cc: leftover debug
    fprintf removed.

  - default paths now target the dscloud reference environment, with the
    VWP/HPC values kept alongside as commented lines. To move between the two,
    comment one block and uncomment the other; ~/.scr_env wins over both.

      variable         dscloud (active)              VWP/HPC (commented)
      ---------------- ----------------------------- ---------------------------
      SPIKE_INSTALL    /share/opt/spike              /user/rocket/opt/spike
      RISCV_TOOLCHAIN  /share/opt/toolchain/         /user/rocket/opt/toolchain/
                       gcc13.2_fastinterrupt         gcc13.2_fastinterrupt
      OUT_PREFIX       $HOME/test_merge_out          /user/rocket/users/dongju2/
      = RISCV                                        test_merge_out
      GCC64_PREFIX     /tools/chipyard/              /user/rocket/chipyard/
                       .conda-env/riscv-tools        .conda-env/riscv-tools
      CHIPYARD         $HOME/_project/chipyard       /user/rocket/users/dongju2/
                                                     _project/chipyard

    Touched: setup_custom_riscv.sh, ci/run_regress.sh, Implementation/Makefile.VERIF
    . the previous defaults pointed at one developer's home directory and could
      not resolve anywhere else; ci/run_regress.sh in particular pointed at a
      path that no longer existed
  - setup_custom_riscv.sh and ci/run_regress.sh now source ~/.scr_env when it
    exists, so a machine that is not the reference environment overrides the
    defaults in one file instead of on every command line.
    Precedence: command-line argument > environment > ~/.scr_env > default.
    See scr_env.example.
  - scr_env.example added (copy to ~/.scr_env and edit)

note on host binaries:
  - spike, vp/spike/verif_*, and the rv32 toolchain are host x86-64 programs and
    must NOT be copied between machines. A spike built on Ubuntu 24.04 requires
    GLIBC_2.33/2.34/2.38, which a conda sysroot does not provide, so the link
    fails with undefined references (fstat@GLIBC_2.33, pthread_join@GLIBC_2.34,
    __libc_single_threaded@GLIBC_2.32). glibc is backward compatible only.
    Build them on the target machine, inside the same environment used to run
    them (activate the chipyard conda env first where one is in use).

